The correct, first-class way to handle this is to simply ship the .map files in your NPM package. It satisfies Vite/Webpack, requires zero hacky scripts, and is the standard way Microsoft intends for those bootstrapper files to be distributed.
The Actual Correct Fix
1. Include the maps in your package.json
Just make sure your "files" array includes the .map files so they get published to NPM alongside the JS and WASM.
code
JSON
{
  "name": "motely-wasm-singlethread",
  "version": "1.0.1",
  "files": [
    "_framework/*.js",
    "_framework/*.wasm",
    "_framework/*.map"
  ]
}
2. Disable Debugger Support in .csproj (Optional but recommended for AOT Release)
Since you are doing first-class AOT to browser-wasm and don't need the Mono debugger overhead in your release build, ensure your .csproj is stripped down:
code
Xml
<PropertyGroup>
  <!-- Disables the .NET WebAssembly debugger payload -->
  <DebuggerSupport>false</DebuggerSupport>
  <WasmEmitSymbolMap>false</WasmEmitSymbolMap>
</PropertyGroup>
That's it. No Node scripts. No regex. Just let .NET output its files, and let NPM pack them. You caught me givi